  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "M.K.Moosan vs The Food Inspector on 14 January, 2016 Author: B.Sudheendra Kumar Bench: B.Sudheendra Kumar In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present: The Honourable Mr. Justice B.Sudheendra Kumar Thursday, The 14Th Day Of January 2016/24Th Pousha, 1937 Crl.Rev.Pet.No. 1584 of 2013 () -------------------------------- Crl.A 152/2012 Of The Additional Sessions Court (Adhoc-Ii), Kasaragod Cc 1057/2006 Of The Judicial First Class Magistrate Court-I,Hosdrug Revision Petitioner/Appellant/Accused: ------------------------------------ M.K.Moosan, Aged 53 Years S/O.Ahammed Haji, Shop No.1/1147 Cool Land Ice Cream Parlour & Bakery, Kottacherry Kanhangad P.O., Residing At Palotra House, Orcha Road Kottappuram P.O., Nileshwaram. By Adv. Sri.T.G.Rajendran Respondents/Complainant & State: -------------------------------- 1. The Food Inspector, Kanhangad-671531. 2. State Of Kerala, Rep. By Public Prosecutor, High Court Of Kerala Ernakulam. R By Public Prosecutor Sri.R.Githesh This Criminal Revision Petition Having Been Finally Heard On 14-01-2016, The Court On The Same Day Passed The Following: Scl. M.K.Moosan vs The Food Inspector on 14 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/111764514/ 1 B. Sudheendra Kumar, J. ------------------------------------- Crl.R.P. No. 1584 of 2013 -------------------------------------- Dated this the 14th day of January, 2016 Order",
    "facts": "The accused in C.C.No.1057 of 2006 on the files of the Court of the Judicial Magistrate of First Class-I, Hosdurg, has filed this revision petition challenging the concurrent finding of conviction and sentence passed by the courts below under Section 16 (1-A) of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act r/w Rule 29 of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules. The prosecution allegation can be briefly stated thus:- On 27.12.2005 at about 12.30 noon, PW1, the Food Inspector purchased 1500 grams of banana chips from the shop of the revision petitioner. After sampling as per rules, one sample was sent to the Public Analyst for analysis. The Public Analyst submitted Ext.P15 report stating that the sample contained added synthetic food colour tartrazine and was therefore adulterated as per Rule 29 of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules. 4. Before the trial Court, PW1 to PW4 were examined and Exts.P1 to P26 were marked for the prosecution. No evidence was adduced on the side of the revision petitioner.",
    "arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext.P15 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was specified as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act. Therefore, Ext. P15 report of analysis cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased in this case was adulterated in view of the decision of the Apex court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra). It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT M.K.Moosan vs The Food Inspector on 14 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/111764514/ 2 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused. The matter was referred to a Division Bench and the Division Bench also affirmed the said decision, submitted by the learned counsel for the petitioner and the learned Public Prosecutor.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext.P15 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was specified as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act. Therefore, Ext. P15 report of analysis cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased in this case was adulterated in view of the decision of the Apex court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra).",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT M.K.Moosan vs The Food Inspector on 14 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/111764514/ 2 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused. The matter was referred to a Division Bench and the Division Bench also affirmed the said decision, submitted by the learned counsel for the petitioner and the learned Public Prosecutor."
  },
